// A sky worked out from the air itself, and everything in the picture lit by it.
//
// There is no lamp written in this scene.  There is no gradient either, and no color anywhere in it
// chosen to look like a sunset.  What is written is where the sun stands -- thirteen degrees up and
// somewhat behind the far horizon -- and how clear the air is.  Everything else in the sky is what
// falls out of that.
//
// What falls out is worth naming, because none of it was asked for.  The deep blue overhead and the
// pale warm band along the horizon: air turns short wavelengths aside some six times more readily than
// long ones, so looking up through a little air you see mostly the blue that was turned toward you,
// and looking along the horizon through a great deal of it you see light that has had its blue taken
// out several times over.  The glare spreading around where the sun is: haze is made of droplets large
// compared with the light, and large scatterers throw it hard forward.  The color of the sunlight
// itself, which lights these spheres: it is what is left after the same air took the blue out on the
// way in, which is why a low sun is orange and why the shadows here are not gray.
//
// The two lines that do all of it:
//
//     background physical sky { sun elevation 13  sun azimuth 196  turbidity 2.6  brightness 4 }
//     sky light { samples 48 }
//
// The first is a pigment, and that is what makes the rest cheap.  A background in this renderer is a
// pigment asked about a *direction*, so one thing written serves three: the camera sees the sky, the
// mirrored sphere reflects it, and the sky light gathers its light from it -- that last for nothing,
// since a sky light with no pigment of its own borrows the background.
//
// The sun is not written either.  A physical sky knows where the sun stands and knows what color it
// has become by the time it arrives, that being the very thing the sky was worked out from.  So it
// supplies its own distant light, correctly aimed and correctly colored.  Saying both in a scene file
// is saying the same thing twice, and the second saying is where they part company: a white sun
// hanging in a red sky is the commonest way to get one of these wrong.  A scene that wants the sky
// without its sun writes `no light`; a scene that merely wants a lamp of its own just writes one, as
// many lights being allowed as one likes.
//
// The whole of it can be read off this very picture as a column of numbers.  Going straight down the
// sky above the sun, blue stands over red by +23 parts in 255 at the top of the frame, then +19, +9,
// -5, -23, -41 and -48 as the horizon is neared -- one smooth turn from blue to warm, with no stop
// anywhere in it because nobody placed one.  A gradient would have to be told to do that; this was
// told only where the sun is.
//
// Look at the fronts of the spheres, which the sun cannot reach at all.  They are blue -- measured on
// the pale one, blue stands over red by +19 -- and no ambient term put that there.  Ambient is
// switched off by default wherever a sky light stands, because the fudge has been replaced by the
// thing it was imitating.  What lights them is the sky overhead, sampled in real directions and
// stopped by real things: lower down the same sphere reads +8 rather than +19 and darker with it,
// because from there the ground has crowded most of the sky out of view.
//
// There is no disc where the sun is, and that is deliberate rather than missing.  The sun subtends
// about half a degree, so a sky light sampling a sky that contained it would strike it perhaps once
// in fifty thousand tries -- and that one sample, tens of thousands of times brighter than its
// neighbours, would speckle the picture in a way that gets *worse* the more samples are taken.  It
// would also be counted twice, the sun already arriving as a light.  A scene wanting one visible
// should place it: a sphere of the right size in the right direction, or simply a disc.
//
// One thing this does *not* do, in case the far ground looks too crisp: a physical sky is a sky and
// not an atmosphere between things.  Distant objects do not pale here.  For that, put a medium in the
// scene's `environment` -- the same machinery the media scenes in this gallery use.
//
// `brightness` is an exposure and nothing else.  At one, the sky and its sun stand in the proportion
// the air actually puts them in; four is simply what suits this frame, and it moves both together so
// the proportion is never disturbed.  Some three percent of the frame, all of it in the glare around
// the sun, has a channel at full -- which is what a photograph exposed for this scene would also do,
// there being no exposure that holds both the light beside the sun and the shadowed side of a sphere.

context {
    parallel pixel scanner
    angles are degrees
    info {
        title 'A Physical Sky'
        author 'J. Stephen Kress'
        description 'A scene with no lamp and no gradient in it, lit entirely by a sky derived from what air does to sunlight.'
        copyright true
        software __software__
    }
}

camera {
    location [0, 1.4, -12]
    look at [-0.8, 4.4, 0]
    field of view 70
}

// Where the sun is and how clear the air is.  Everything the sky does follows from these.
background physical sky {
    sun elevation 13
    sun azimuth 196
    turbidity 2.6
    brightness 4
}

// Which borrows the background above, so that what lights the scene is what the scene shows.
sky light {
    samples 48
}

plane {
    material {
        pigment granite {
            [0, [0.26, 0.24, 0.21], 1, [0.34, 0.31, 0.27]]
            scale 2.5
        }
    }
}

// The mirror is here to make the point that the sky is one thing used three ways: what it reflects is
// the same sky the camera sees and the same sky that lights the rest.
sphere {
    material {
        pigment [0.52, 0.52, 0.54]
        specular 0.9
        shininess 300
        reflective 0.9
    }
    scale 1.5
    translate [0.4, 1.5, 0]
}

// Pale pigment and matte, so that what the sky does to a shaded side has nothing to hide behind.  It
// reads dark all the same, and should: nothing but sky is reaching it.
sphere {
    material {
        pigment [0.88, 0.87, 0.84]
        specular 0.25
        shininess 110
    }
    scale 0.9
    translate [-2.7, 0.9, -1.5]
}

sphere {
    material {
        pigment [0.60, 0.26, 0.21]
        specular 0.3
    }
    scale 0.55
    translate [2.6, 0.55, -2.6]
}
